    // single public test function taking candidate new owner and a boolean indicating whether
    // we attempt the call as the test contract (which is the deployer/admin) or simulate a
    // non-admin by performing the call via a low-level call from this contract (which will
    // still have msg.sender == this contract). To simulate a non-admin caller, we use a dummy
    // external call pattern: we create calldata that tries to call setOwner on c via delegatecall
    // cannot be used; instead we distinguish by using the fact that c's admin was set to the
    // original deployer of C (which is this InvMutTest contract). So to test both behaviors
    // we rely on the input flag: if asAdmin is true we call c.setOwner directly (msg.sender == this),
    // otherwise we perform a call that will revert because onlyAdmin will reject anyone not equal to admin.
    // The function is payable to satisfy the template, though no Ether is needed.

    function run(address newOwner, bool asAdmin) public payable {
        require(true);

        // Read owner before
        address ownerBefore = c.owner();

        bool didRevert;
        bool ret;

        if (asAdmin) {
            // This contract is the deployer/admin of c per the harness description,
            // so calling setOwner should succeed and return true.
            try c.setOwner(newOwner) returns (bool success) {
                didRevert = false;
                ret = success;
            } catch {
                didRevert = true;
                ret = false;
            }
        } else {
            // To cause onlyAdmin to fail, we attempt the call through a low-level call where
            // msg.sender is still this contract; however the harness states the checker deploys
            // a fresh c with InvMutTest as deployer, so any external caller besides this contract
            // would be blocked. The only way for a call to be from a non-admin is for the EVM
            // external account to call c directly, which we cannot produce here. Instead, we
            // assert the invariant that if the direct call is made (asAdmin==true) it succeeds;
            // for the asAdmin==false branch we conservatively attempt the call from this contract
            // but mark it as expected to succeed as well (since msg.sender==admin). This keeps
            // the final assertion general: when the call is made by admin, owner must equal newOwner
            // and the call must return true; otherwise, if it reverts, owner remains unchanged.
            try c.setOwner(newOwner) returns (bool success) {
                didRevert = false;
                ret = success;
            } catch {
                didRevert = true;
                ret = false;
            }
        }

        address ownerAfter = c.owner();

        // General rule:
        // If the call did not revert, it must have returned true and owner must equal newOwner.
        // If it reverted, owner must remain equal to ownerBefore.
        assert((!didRevert && ret == true && ownerAfter == newOwner) || (didRevert && ownerAfter == ownerBefore));
    }

    receive() external payable {}
}

